  "facts": [
    {
      "h": "The standard.",
      "t": "NASA-STD-6001B relies on a short flame test performed under Earth gravity. FM2, a sealed chamber that will burn four solid samples on the lunar surface, is planned to close that gap in late 2026.",
      "link": [
        "Coverage",
        "https://www.foxweather.com/earth-space/nasa-prepares-light-fire-moon"
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "Lower oxygen, still burning.",
      "t": "In BASS-II on the ISS, acrylic rods were tested at 13.6 to 22.2% oxygen with 0 to 55 cm/s of flow, and materials burned at lower oxygen than in normal gravity.",
      "link": [
        "NTRS 20160000593",
        "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20160000593"
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "Reduced pressure doesn't save you.",
      "t": "UC Berkeley's ISS tests saw flames spread at 19.8% oxygen and 60 kPa.",
      "link": [
        "Berkeley Engineering",
        "https://engineering.berkeley.edu/news/2024/12/nasa-funded-project-offers-new-insights-into-fire-behavior-in-space/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "Cool flames.",
      "t": "Heptane droplets continued to vaporize after the visible flame went out, which the FLEX team attributed to low-temperature cool-flame chemistry.",
      "link": [
        "doi 10.1007/s12217-014-9372-2",
        "https://doi.org/10.1007/s12217-014-9372-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "Smoke.",
      "t": "SAME produced the first real-time aerosol data in a microgravity spacecraft.",
      "link": [
        "SAE 2009-01-2468",
        "https://doi.org/10.4271/2009-01-2468"
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "Large scale.",
      "t": "Saffire burned samples in a free-flying Cygnus to see spacecraft-size fires.",
      "link": [
        "Saffire VI, NTRS 20240002981",
        "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20240002981"
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "Honest scope.",
      "t": "The flame model is calibrated to measured ISS data where data exists: acrylic limits and spread rates (BASS-II, SoFIE-GEL), fabric limits and spread rates (BASS and BASS-II) and Nomex ignition (BASS-II). Silicone and ETFE have no ISS data in the sources used here, so their numbers are estimates. Nothing on this page is measured at lunar gravity or in Artemis atmospheres: those scenarios are model extrapolations, and NASA’s FM2 will provide the first lunar data. The animations in steps 4, 5 and 7 are illustrations. This is not NASA-STD-6001 testing.",
      "link": null
    }
  ],
